<exploration>
    <title>Irrational Numbers in the Real World</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-real-life-irrational" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/real-life-irrational.js">
        <slate xml:id="jxgbox-real-life" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Drag the <term>Scale Factor</term> slider away from <m>1.618</m> to make it a simpler rational decimal (like <m>1.500</m> or <m>2.000</m>). Notice how the curve and shape change.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Carefully return the slider back to <m>1.618</m>. Why do plants rely on the infinite, non-repeating nature of the irrational number <m>\phi = \frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}</m> to pack leaves and seeds perfectly without overlaps?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration>
