    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
    <statement>
        <p>A carpenter is reinforcing a square gate with an area of 18 <m>\text{m}^2</m>. Find the exact length of one side.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
             Since the area of a square is <m>\text{Area} = s^2</m>, the side length is found by calculating the square root of the area, <m>s = \sqrt{\text{Area}}</m>:
            <md>
                <mrow>s \amp = \sqrt{18}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{9 \times 2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 3\sqrt{2}\text{ meters}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>

<example>
    <statement>
        <p>Find the exact diagonal of a football pitch goalpost that is 2m high and 6m wide.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            According to the Pythagorean theorem, the diagonal of a rectangle is calculated using the formula <m>\text{diagonal} = \sqrt{\text{height}^2 + \text{width}^2}</m>:
            <md>
                <mrow>d \amp = \sqrt{2^2 + 6^2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{4 + 36}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{40}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{4 \times 10} = 2\sqrt{10}\text{ meters}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>

<example>
    <statement>
        <p>A square classroom floor has an area of 32 <m>\text{m}^2</m>. Find the exact length of one side.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Because the area of a square is determined by squaring its side length (<m>\text{Area} = s^2</m>), the exact side length equals <m>s = \sqrt{\text{Area}}</m>:
            <md>
                <mrow>s \amp = \sqrt{32}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{16 \times 2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 4\sqrt{2}\text{ meters}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>

<example>
    <statement>
        <p>A ladder is placed against a wall. The base is 3 m from the wall and the height reached is 5 m. Find the exact length of the ladder.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Treating the ladder as a hypotenuse, we apply the Pythagorean theorem formula, where <m>\text{length} = \sqrt{\text{base}^2 + \text{height}^2}</m>:
            <md>
                <mrow>d \amp = \sqrt{3^2 + 5^2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{9 + 25}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{34}\text{ meters}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>

<example>
    <statement>
        <p>A square school field has a side length of <m>\sqrt{50}</m> meters. Express this length in simplest surd form.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            To convert a length to its simplest surd form, evaluate the number under the radical using the factoring rule <m>\sqrt{a \times b} = \sqrt{a} \times \sqrt{b}</m>, targeting the largest perfect square:
            <md>
                <mrow>\sqrt{50} \amp = \sqrt{25 \times 2}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 5\sqrt{2}\text{ meters}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>

<!-- Expanded Exercises -->

<exercises>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>A square notice board has an area of 72 <m>\text{cm}^2</m>. Find the exact side length.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using the geometric property of squares where <m>\text{Area} = s^2</m>, we find the exact side using <m>s = \sqrt{\text{Area}}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>s \amp = \sqrt{72}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{36 \times 2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 6\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>The diagonal of a rectangular classroom is formed by sides of 6 m and 8 m. Find the exact diagonal length.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                By applying the Pythagorean theorem, the straight line diagonal across a rectangle satisfies the formula <m>\text{diagonal} = \sqrt{\text{side}_1^2 + \text{side}_2^2}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>d \amp = \sqrt{6^2 + 8^2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{36 + 64}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{100} = 10\text{ m}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>A square garden has an area of 98 <m>\text{m}^2</m>. Find the exact side length in simplest form.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Knowing that the formula for a square's boundary layout is derived from <m>\text{Area} = s^2</m>, the side calculation operates via <m>s = \sqrt{\text{Area}}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>s \amp = \sqrt{98}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{49 \times 2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 7\sqrt{2}\text{ m}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>Simplify the length of a rope measuring <m>\sqrt{180}</m> cm.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To simplify this line value, isolate the highest square factor from the value under the radical using the identity <m>\sqrt{a \times b} = \sqrt{a} \times \sqrt{b}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{180} \amp = \sqrt{36 \times 5}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 6\sqrt{5}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>A square tile has a side length of <m>\sqrt{27}</m> cm. If two tiles are placed side by side, find the total length.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, establish the single dimension by reducing the radical using the identity <m>\sqrt{a \times b} = \sqrt{a}\sqrt{b}</m>:
                <me>\text{Tile Side} = \sqrt{27} = \sqrt{9 \times 3} = 3\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}</me>
                Next, determine the adjacent measurement by using the arithmetic addition rule <m>\text{Total Length} = 2 \times \text{Tile Side}</m>:
                <me>2 \times 3\sqrt{3} = 6\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>A rectangular field has length 7 m and width 9 m. Find the exact diagonal length.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using the structural corner math of the Pythagorean theorem, the right-angle diagonal edge matches the formula <m>\text{diagonal} = \sqrt{\text{length}^2 + \text{width}^2}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>d \amp = \sqrt{7^2 + 9^2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{49 + 81}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{130}\text{ m}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>A square has a side length of <m>\sqrt{20}</m> cm. Express it in simplest surd form.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Simplify this geometric span by pulling out the highest matching perfect square factor according to the basic radical properties:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{20} \amp = \sqrt{4 \times 5}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2\sqrt{5}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>If a wire of length <m>\sqrt{200}</m> cm is cut into 2 equal parts, find the length of each part in simplest form.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, break down the baseline length using the perfect square factoring method:
                <me>\text{Total Length} = \sqrt{200} = \sqrt{100 \times 2} = 10\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}</me>
                Then, divide the full measure across two equal section segments using the algebraic formula <m>\text{Segment Length} = \frac{\text{Total Length}}{2}</m>:
                <me>\frac{10\sqrt{2}}{2} = 5\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>A decorative triangular window has a base of 4m and a height of 4m. Calculate the exact length of the slanted side (hypotenuse).</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using the right-triangle framework defined by the Pythagorean theorem, the hypotenuse profile length follows the formula <m>\text{hypotenuse} = \sqrt{\text{base}^2 + \text{height}^2}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>h \amp = \sqrt{4^2 + 4^2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{16 + 16}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{32} = \sqrt{16 \times 2} = 4\sqrt{2}\text{ meters}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>If a wire of length <m>\sqrt{108}</m> cm is cut into 3 equal pieces, find the exact length of each piece in simplest form.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, parse the initial system length value into simpler radical values using square factoring:
                <me>\text{Total Length} = \sqrt{108} = \sqrt{36 \times 3} = 6\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}</me>
                Next, parse the line split allocation using the mathematical breakdown rule <m>\text{Piece Length} = \frac{\text{Total Length}}{3}</m>:
                <me>\frac{6\sqrt{3}}{3} = 2\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</exercises>
